    1714	### D8 — Differentiable architecture search (DARTS) for π(x) circuits
    1715	
    1716	**Question:** §A.A1 (S84) used SAT/ILP to enumerate small TC⁰
    1717	circuits computing PRIMES at N=8, finding that depth-2 sign-threshold
    1718	W=1 circuits need ≥ 17 gates AND that PRIMES has a statistically
    1719	significant 1-bit-predictor advantage over matched random functions
    1720	(p < 0.001 at N=6). The SAT approach is provably intractable beyond
    1721	N ≈ 10. Differentiable Architecture Search (Liu-Simonyan-Yang 2019
    1722	ICLR) replaces discrete circuit enumeration with a continuous
    1723	relaxation: each gate is a softmax-mixture of `{AND, OR, XOR, MAJ_k,
    1724	ID}` operations, weights are trained by gradient descent on the
    1725	`(n, χ_P(n))` regression loss, then the architecture is discretised
    1726	to the highest-weight operations. DARTS reaches search spaces of size
    1727	10^4 gates that SAT cannot. Question: does DARTS, applied to a
    1728	fixed-depth threshold-circuit search space at N=12, 14, 16, find
    1729	low-loss circuits computing PRIMES that EXTRAPOLATE to held-out
    1730	N ∈ {20, 24}? If yes, the discovered circuits are CANDIDATE evidence
    1731	for PRIMES ∈ TC⁰; their structure can be inspected and potentially
    1732	formalised.
    1733	
    1734	**Why frontier:** **no published work applies neural architecture
    1735	search to primality**. The S84 SAT approach is intrinsically
    1736	size-limited (exponential blow-up at N ≥ 10). DARTS is the canonical
    1737	bridge between discrete circuit synthesis and continuous optimisation,
    1738	achieving 10^4-gate searches in hours where SAT would take centuries.
    1739	Two outcomes are both informative: (i) DARTS finds a low-loss
    1740	generalising architecture → CANDIDATE TC⁰ circuit, structural
    1741	inspection could yield a proof; (ii) DARTS provably fails (loss
    1742	plateaus, no extrapolation) → empirical *circuit-search lower bound*
    1743	complementary to S84, in a much larger search space.
